# Generic functions

Some functions work the same way for every element type. The last element of
a list is found the same way whether the list holds titles or page counts. A
**generic function** is written once with a **type parameter**, a capital
letter in angle brackets after the name, and each call fills it in:

```hql
fn last_of<T>(xs: List<T>) : Option<T> = xs[-1]
[last_of(["Dune", "Emma"]), last_of([] | List<String>)]
```

```text
["Emma", none]
```

Called with a `List<String>`, `T` is `String` and the result is an
`Option<String>`. Called with a `List<Int>`, the result is an `Option<Int>`:

<!-- run: check -->
```hql
fn last_of<T>(xs: List<T>) : Option<T> = xs[-1]
last_of([412, 280])
```

```text
Option<Int>
```

## Bounds

Inside the body, `T` can be any type, so the body can only do what works
for every type. Finding the greatest element needs an order, and not every
type has one. The checker rejects the body:

```hql
fn longest<T>(xs: List<T>) : Option<T> = xs | max
longest([412, 280])
```

```text
check error[unsatisfied-bound]: `max` needs elements with an order of their own, and T has none; sort with an explicit key first
 --> <expression>:1:47
  |
1 | fn longest<T>(xs: List<T>) : Option<T> = xs | max
  |                                               ^^^
```

A **bound** restricts the type parameter. `T : Orderable` says `T` has an
order, so the body may use `max`, `min` and `sort`:

```hql
fn longest<T : Orderable>(xs: List<T>) : Option<T> = xs | max
longest([412, 280, 310])
```

```text
412
```

The bound is now part of the contract, and each call is checked against it.
A set has no order of its own, so a list of sets is rejected:

```hql
fn longest<T : Orderable>(xs: List<T>) : Option<T> = xs | max
longest([{1}, {2}])
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `longest` needs T : Orderable, and T is Set<Int> here
 --> <expression>:2:1
  |
2 | longest([{1}, {2}])
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

A bound is a type or a trait. [[traits|Traits]] shows how to declare traits
and use them as bounds.
